+# Base Fork Notes
+
+The onboarding note is wrong about both the fork command and the sanity check.
+
+This repo's root script only delegates to the Foundry workspace:
+
+```jsonc
+// package.json
+"fork": "yarn foundry:fork",
+"foundry:fork": "yarn workspace @se-2/foundry fork"
+```
+
+The Foundry workspace script is:
+
+```jsonc
+// packages/foundry/package.json
+"fork": "make fork FORK_URL=${1:-mainnet}"
+```
+
+and the Makefile starts Anvil with that `FORK_URL` while forcing the local chain id:
+
+```make
+# packages/foundry/Makefile
+fork: setup-anvil-wallet
+	anvil --fork-url ${FORK_URL} --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+`yarn fork base` does not put `base` in the script's `$1`, so `${1:-mainnet}` falls back to `mainnet`. That starts a local fork of Ethereum mainnet, not Base. The local node still responds and mines transactions because Anvil is running correctly, but the upstream state is wrong.
+
+That is why reads against Base USDC at `0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913` come back empty. On Ethereum mainnet there is no contract code at that Base USDC address (`cast code ... --rpc-url https://ethereum-rpc.publicnode.com` returns `0x`), so calls to it are calls to an empty account. On Base, the same address has real token code and returns `symbol() == "USDC"` and `decimals() == 6`.
+
+## Correct Command
+
+From the repo root, use:
+
+```bash
+yarn fork --network base
+```
+
+Use that exact spaced form. `yarn fork --network=base` has the same problem as `yarn fork base` here: it does not put `base` in `$1`, so the script falls back to `mainnet`.
+
+The `base` name is defined in this checkout's Foundry config:
+
+```toml
+# packages/foundry/foundry.toml
+[rpc_endpoints]
+base = "https://mainnet.base.org"
+```
+
+With the argument in the right position, the Makefile command resolves to a Base fork:
+
+```bash
+anvil --fork-url base --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+## Sanity Check
+
+Do not use `cast chain-id` as the fork sanity check. This repo always starts the fork with `--chain-id 31337`, so chain id only proves that a local Anvil node is answering. It does not prove which chain Anvil forked.
+
+Check Base-specific state instead:
+
+```bash
+cast call 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 \
+  "decimals()(uint8)" \
+  --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+```
+
+Expected output on the correct Base fork:
+
+```text
+6
+```
+
+An equivalent code-presence check is:
+
+```bash
+cast code 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 \
+  --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+```
+
+Expected output on the correct Base fork is non-empty bytecode, not `0x`.

